# Installation

This page adds Kiln to an existing Rails app and runs a first build. The current release is <%= Kiln::VERSION %>.

## 1. Add the gem

```ruby
# Gemfile
gem "kiln"
```

Run `bundle install`. There is no generator: the gem adds its rake tasks when the app loads.

## 2. Keep app/assets/builds

Propshaft only serves asset folders that exist when the app boots. Track `app/assets/builds/.keep` in version control, as a new Rails app does. Without it, a build on a fresh checkout stops with an error that says so.

## 3. Write config/kiln.rb

```ruby
# config/kiln.rb
Kiln.configure do |config|
  config.url = "https://example.com"
  config.not_found = "/404"
end

Kiln.pages do
  params :post, -> { Post.published.map { { slug: it.slug } } }
  skip :search, reason: "dynamic"
end
```

`url` is the site's public address, and `sitemap.xml` is written only when it is set. `not_found` is the route that becomes `404.html`. [Configuration](/configuration/) lists every option.

`Kiln.pages` says what to do with routes that have params. Leave it out if no route has any. If you are not sure which routes need it, run a build: the errors give the lines to add. Param sources are lambdas, so they can name models that are not loaded yet when this file loads.

## 4. Build

```sh
RAILS_ENV=production bin/rails kiln:build
```

Set `RAILS_ENV` yourself for a site you deploy. Rails picks the gem groups before the task starts, so the task cannot set it.

The site goes to `dist/`. Add `dist/` to `.gitignore`, unless you [commit the output](/deploying/#committed-output) on purpose. From the second build on, the output lists what changed:

```text
Kiln build succeeded: 9 files written.
Pages since last build: 0 added, 0 removed, 1 changed
  changed: /about
Output: /path/to/app/dist (production, 0.1s)
```

If a build fails, the [error reference](/build/#errors) explains each message. Set `VERBOSE=1` to see the request log.

## 5. Look at the output

```sh
bin/rails kiln:serve
```

This serves `dist/` at `http://localhost:4000`, close to how a static host does. Set `PORT` and `BIND` to change the address. It is for checking a build. To work on pages, use `bin/rails server`.

Next, [deploy the output](/deploying/), or read about [pages](/pages/) to declare the rest of your routes.
